e that's  the coroner's job to determine if a  crime was committed back in medieval  times however they're really much more  of a glorified tax collector and what  that means is that if somebody dies and  their body was unclaimed and you run  across it you must raise the human cry  and the reason why you do this is  because the government wants to collect  any bits that the dead person might have  laid on the floor or if a crime was  committed they'd like to claim the  dagger that killed you and sell it on to  raise taxes so to give an example and  this is a completely this was not a real  person although I should point out you  can't libel the dead so let's say you  know Henri de Buckingham Clarke died of  a stab wound value of knives one  shilling 1327 and if any of you are on  Twitter there's an account called  medieval despot that just basically  tweets things like this automatically  and if you're wondering quite a lot of  things quite a lot of people get called  Clarke simply because it's anybody whose  profession involves writing including  students so you're thinking to yourself  like why is somebody who works at the  7-eleven going around and stabbing so  many people it's actually because the  word had a much broader meaning Xin  still at the same time we're only  recording certain types of deaths for  certain reasons this kind of also falls  out of use as the role of the coroner  starts to change the one that people  know about and the one that's really  much more directly related to how we  document death now are the Bills of  mortality the first ones are in 1592 and  obviously we know what's going on around  that time a whole lot of people are  dying really quickly because they're  sticking their dicks and toasters aka  plagues flags are happening and when a  plague is active let's say it's  summertime and the plaguing is easy  everybody in the city is dying a bill of  mortality will be issued and it lists by  parish how many people died so in the  city 